---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/docs/Callout.astro";
import LinkCards from "../../components/docs/LinkCards.astro";
import Diagram from "../../components/docs/Diagram.astro";
import { memoryDiagram } from "../../data/docs/diagrams";
import { docHref } from "../../data/docs";

const sections = [
  { id: "what", title: "What a memory is" },
  { id: "turn-on", title: "Turn memory on" },
  { id: "made", title: "How memories are made" },
  { id: "review", title: "Review and edit memories" },
  { id: "later-runs", title: "How later runs use memory" },
  { id: "models", title: "Choose who summarizes runs" },
  { id: "privacy", title: "What leaves your computer" },
  { id: "next-steps", title: "Next steps" },
];
---

<DocsLayout slug="memory" sections={sections}>
  <section aria-labelledby="what">
    <h2 id="what">What a memory is</h2>
    <p>
      A memory is a short note about a project that's worth knowing next time, such as a decision and its reason, an approach that failed, or a command that works. OpenOrc adds the best-ranked ones to the instructions of each new
      run in the project, so agents start from what earlier runs learned.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each memory has a type, a title, a body, and a confidence from 0.1 to 1, and it can name the files it concerns. The types are Decision, Lesson, Command, Env quirk, Convention, Preference, Spec, and Ownership. Memories belong to
      a project, and every agent working there can read them. Orclings keep a private memory of their own; see <a href={docHref("orclings")}>Orclings</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="turn-on">
    <h2 id="turn-on">Turn memory on</h2>
    <p>Memory is off in a new profile. One switch turns it on for all your projects.</p>
    <ol class="docs-steps">
      <li>Click <strong>Settings</strong> at the bottom of the sidebar, then <strong>Memory &amp; models</strong>.</li>
      <li>Turn on the switch next to <strong>OpenOrc memory is off</strong>.</li>
      <li>Read the line under the heading. It names the agent and model that will summarize your finished runs.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Turning memory off stops OpenOrc from saving or recalling memories. The memories you have stay, and you can still edit and delete them.</p>
    <Callout>The switch covers OpenOrc's memory only. An agent's own memory features, such as Claude Code's auto memory, are managed in that agent.</Callout>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="made">
    <h2 id="made">How memories are made</h2>
    <p>Memories come from two places:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        After a run, a small model reads a digest of it and saves up to six memories, plus a summary of what was asked, what was done, and what was left open. These memories are marked <strong>Learned after a run</strong>.
      </li>
      <li>An agent can save one while it works. These are marked <strong>Saved by an agent</strong>, and the transcript shows a <strong>Remembered</strong> step.</li>
    </ul>
    <Diagram diagram={memoryDiagram} />
    <p>
      A run is over when the agent's process exits. In a conversation, that happens once the agent has been idle for the time in <strong>Close idle processes after</strong> (<strong>Settings</strong> → <strong>General</strong>, 10
      minutes by default), so a conversation is usually summarized once per working session.
    </p>
    <p>When a new memory has the same topic as an existing one, it updates that memory and raises its confidence by 0.1.</p>
    <Callout>
      OpenOrc skips task discussions, conversations that never finished a turn, and runs still open when you quit OpenOrc. It doesn't retry a summary that failed. With <strong>Automatic · per agent</strong>, OpenCode runs
      aren't summarized.
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="review">
    <h2 id="review">Review and edit memories</h2>
    <ol class="docs-steps">
      <li>In the sidebar, open <strong>More</strong> and choose <strong>Memory</strong>. To keep it in the sidebar, choose <strong>Edit sidebar…</strong> in the same menu.</li>
      <li>Choose a project in the menu next to <strong>Saved memories</strong>.</li>
      <li>Type in <strong>Search active memories</strong>, or filter by type and by who saved them.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Each memory shows who saved it and when it was created, updated, and last confirmed. Its three-dot menu has these actions:</p>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Action</th>
            <th scope="col">What it does</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Edit</strong></td>
            <td>Change the title and details, then click <strong>Save</strong>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Mark helpful</strong></td>
            <td>Raises its confidence by 0.1 and counts as a fresh confirmation, so it ranks higher.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Add to CLAUDE.md</strong>, <strong>Add to AGENTS.md</strong></td>
            <td>Adds it as a bullet under a "Project memory (from OpenOrc)" heading in that file, in the project's root folder.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Retract</strong></td>
            <td>Marks it as wrong. Agents stop seeing it, and it stays in the list, dimmed.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Delete</strong></td>
            <td>Removes it.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      In a conversation, the side panel's <strong>Memory</strong> tab lists the project's memories once there are any. A task's <strong>Memory</strong> tab shows the same list, except on an agent team's assignment, where it
      shows <strong>Run history</strong> and <strong>Memory from this task</strong>. Workspace conversations, Slack ones included, share one memory that the project menu doesn't list; open the <strong>Memory</strong> tab in a
      Workspace conversation to see it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="later-runs">
    <h2 id="later-runs">How later runs use memory</h2>
    <p>
      When memory is on and the project has memories, OpenOrc adds a project memory section to the <a href={docHref("instructions") + "#brief"}>brief each run gets</a>. It holds up to 6 decisions, 8 lessons, 6 commands, 4 env
      quirks, 5 conventions, and 5 preferences, ranked by confidence and by how recently each was confirmed. Spec and Ownership memories stay out. The section also lists tasks in progress or in review, and items that recent runs left open. Each
      line shows its age and who saved it, and the section stops at 5,000 characters.
    </p>
    <p>
      OpenOrc builds the brief when an agent's process starts, so a new memory reaches a conversation the next time its agent starts. During a run, agents can also search memory by words and by meaning with <code
        >memory_search</code
      >, save a memory with <code>memory_record</code>, and mark one helpful, wrong, or stale with <code>memory_feedback</code>. A run on a task can call <code>task_context</code> for the task's spec, its earlier runs, and the most
      relevant memories.
    </p>
    <p>
      Saving and rating follow the conversation's mode: Plan blocks them, Review everything asks you first, and Accept edits and Autonomous allow them. See <a href={docHref("permissions") + "#openorc-tools"}>OpenOrc's tools</a>.
      While memory is off, agents get only <code>task_context</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="models">
    <h2 id="models">Choose who summarizes runs</h2>
    <p>
      In <strong>Settings</strong> → <strong>Memory &amp; models</strong>, the <strong>Learn from completed runs</strong> section decides who writes the summaries. With memory on, choose a <strong>Provider</strong>:
    </p>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Provider</th>
            <th scope="col">Who summarizes</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Automatic · per agent</strong></td>
            <td>
              The agent that did the work, with a small model: Claude Haiku 4.5 for Claude Code, and for Codex the first model whose name contains spark, mini, or nano (Codex's default if none does). This is the default.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Codex subscription</strong>, <strong>Claude Code subscription</strong></td>
            <td>That agent summarizes every run, whichever agent did the work. Pick its <strong>Model</strong> or keep <strong>Default</strong>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Anthropic API key</strong></td>
            <td>Claude Code with a key you enter and save with <strong>Save API key</strong>. The key is billed separately from a Claude subscription.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Off · no background learning</strong></td>
            <td>Nothing is summarized. Agents can still save and search memories.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Except with an API key, the agent that summarizes must be signed in. The line under the section says who will summarize runs, or why no one can. <strong>Text generation</strong>, on the same page, picks the model that names
      new threads and works separately from memory.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="privacy">
    <h2 id="privacy">What leaves your computer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Memories, run summaries, and the search index stay in OpenOrc's data folder. Searches run on your computer.</li>
      <li>
        Searching by meaning needs a small embedding model. The first time memory needs it, OpenOrc downloads it (about 83 MB, from <code>storage.googleapis.com</code>) and checks it against a fixed checksum. Nothing is downloaded
        while memory is off.
      </li>
      <li>
        Each summary sends a digest of the run to the summarizing provider under your account: your messages, changed files, recent tool calls, and the agent's last messages, up to 12,000 characters. It comes from OpenOrc's
        stored copy of the run, which has been through a best-effort filter for well-known secrets such as API keys.
      </li>
      <li>With <strong>Automatic · per agent</strong>, a run's digest goes only to the provider that ran it. A provider you choose receives every run's digest.</li>
    </ul>
    <Callout>Memories move between agents. A lesson saved from a Claude Code run can appear in the brief of a later Codex run in the same project.</Callout>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="next-steps">
    <h2 id="next-steps">Next steps</h2>
    <LinkCards cards={[{ slug: "instructions" }, { slug: "permissions" }, { slug: "orclings" }, { slug: "storage" }]} />
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
